trings are represented inline as the i64 (ptr << 32) | len (see\\n// gen_str_concat_body); the bytes live at ptr in the bump heap. The previous\\n// implementation accumulated `acc = concat(acc, sep); acc = concat(acc,\\n// arr[i])`, which copied the whole prefix each step — O(n^2) time and O(n^2)\\n// bump garbage (the streaming bench's dominant cost, and an OOM/out-of-bounds\\n// risk at ~4 KB; see scripts/bench_streaming.sh). Instead: pass 1 sums the\\n// total length, allocate the result once, pass 2 memcpy's each piece (and the\\n// separator) into place — O(n). This also speeds up StringBuilder::freeze,\\n// which lowers to String::join(parts, \\\"\\\").\\n//\\n// Locals (params 0=arr, 1=sep are i64): i32 group [2..10] = n, i, total,\\n// sep_len, sep_ptr, base, pos, cur_ptr, cur_len; i64 group [11] = cur. Keep\\n// meta_i32=9 / meta_i64=1 in linked_compile in sync. arr_len/arr_get use the\\n// untagged convention (matching the prior body); ptr/len are extracted exactly\\n// as gen_str_concat_body does.\\nexport fn gen_string_join_body(enable_rc: Bool, arr_len_idx: Int, arr_get_idx: Int, str_concat_idx: Int) -> Bytes {\\n  let _ = str_concat_idx\\n  let b = bytebuf_new()\\n  // n = arr_len(arr). Under RC `arr_len` returns the length TAGGED (len<<1) and\\n  // `arr_get` expects the index TAGGED (#705) — this body otherwise uses the raw\\n  // untagged convention, so untag the count here and tag each index below, or\\n  // the loop runs 2*len times (OOB) and reads element i/2.\\n  emit_local_get(b, 0)\\n  emit_call(b, arr_len_idx)\\n  if enable_rc {\\n    emit_i64_const(b, 1)\\n    emit_i64_shr_s(b)\\n  } else {\\n    ()\\n  }\\n  emit_i32_wrap_i64(b)\\n  emit_local_set(b, 2)\\n  // empty array -> empty string (0)\\n  emit_local_get(b, 2)\\n  emit_i32_eqz(b)\\n  emit_if_i64(b)\\n  emit_i64_const(b, 0)\\n  emit_else(b)\\n  // sep_len = lo32(sep); sep_ptr = hi32(sep)\\n  emit_local_get(b, 1)\\n  emit_i64_const(b, 4294967295)\\n  emit_i64_and(b)\\n  emit_i32_wrap_i